{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The fallback that outlived the bug</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A dashboard widget crashed whenever the websocket delivered a malformed price update — a genuine, occasional bug on the backend. The frontend team wrapped it in a React error boundary, which is exactly the right instinct: instead of a blank white screen, users saw a calm "Something went wrong" card. Shipped, tested, closed the ticket.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then the backend bug got fixed a few seconds later, and the feed started sending valid data again. The widget never came back. Every user who'd hit the crash once kept seeing "Something went wrong" for the rest of their session, even though the data feeding it was fine again — the only way back was a full page reload.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The error boundary did exactly what its <code>getDerivedStateFromError</code>/<code>componentDidCatch</code> contract promises: it caught the render-phase exception and swapped in fallback UI instead of unmounting the whole app. What it didn't do — because nothing told it to — was ever attempt to render the real subtree again. <code>hasError</code> stayed <code>true</code> in the boundary's own state for the rest of that component instance's lifetime, so every subsequent re-render, no matter what changed upstream, rendered the same fallback branch.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is the gap between "catches an error" and "recovers from an error," and it's easy to miss in code review because the fallback UI looks like success — no crash, no blank screen, a friendly message. But a boundary with no reset path degrades every subsequent render of that subtree to the fallback for the lifetime of the component, regardless of whether the triggering condition still holds. It quietly converts a transient error into a permanent one.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Give the boundary a reason to try again</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A React error boundary is a component that implements <code>static getDerivedStateFromError()</code> (to flip into fallback rendering) and <code>componentDidCatch()</code> (to log or report). Once that internal flag is set, React keeps rendering the fallback branch — there's no built-in timer or condition that flips it back automatically. Recovery means something external gives the boundary a reason to attempt a real mount again: either changing the <code>key</code> prop on the boundary (React treats a new key as "this is a different component instance," so it discards the old tree — including the error state — and mounts a fresh one), or calling an explicit reset function — like the <code>resetErrorBoundary()</code> the popular <code>react-error-boundary</code> library hands to your fallback — from a "Try again" button, which clears <code>hasError</code> and re-attempts the render.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Two real mechanisms, same underlying idea: force a fresh mount attempt instead of continuing to render a stale caught-error branch. Bumping the key (<code>&lt;ErrorBoundary key={connectionId}&gt;</code>) makes React discard and remount the whole subtree whenever <code>connectionId</code> changes — useful when you already have a natural signal that the underlying condition changed. Calling a reset callback from a "Try again" button clears the boundary's internal error state and re-renders children directly — useful when the user, not a prop, should control the retry. <code>react-error-boundary</code>'s <code>resetKeys</code> prop combines both: pass an array, and the boundary auto-resets whenever any entry in it changes, without you hand-wiring the comparison.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Both mechanisms produce a genuine new render attempt of the previously-crashing children — not a cached "looks fixed" state. That matters: if the underlying cause hasn't actually changed, the fresh attempt throws again and the boundary re-catches it, which is correct behavior, not a bug in the reset. The dangerous version is a reset that fires unconditionally — on a timer, or on every parent re-render — which produces a crash-reset-crash loop that can pin the main thread or spam an error-reporting service. A <code>resetKeys</code> array (or an explicit, user-triggered button) that only fires on an actual state change is the safer default over an automatic interval retry.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The retry button that told the truth</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A different team building the same kind of live-data widget added a "Try again" button inside their error boundary's fallback from the start. The button called a reset function that cleared the caught-error flag and rendered the widget's children fresh. The first time a user hit the same malformed-data bug and clicked "Try again" immediately, the widget crashed a second time — because the bad data was still arriving. That's expected: the button gives a real second chance, it doesn't guarantee success.
    </p>
    <p>
      A few seconds later, once the backend actually recovered, the same user clicked "Try again" once more, and the widget rendered correctly — no reload, no lost scroll position, no lost session.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The interesting design detail is that the second crash wasn't a failure of the pattern — a boundary reset is supposed to attempt a real render and let it fail again if the cause is still active. What made this version trustworthy was pairing the manual "Try again" button with a <code>resetKeys</code> entry tied to the websocket's last-message timestamp: even without the user clicking anything, a genuinely new message arriving reset the boundary automatically, so recovery didn't strictly depend on someone noticing and clicking.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This also fixed an error-reporting problem the previous team didn't have a name for yet: <code>componentDidCatch</code> fired once per crash, which is correct, but a version with no working reset meant one root cause generated exactly one report per user even though the fallback then displayed for the rest of the session — undercounting how many render attempts users were actually blocked on. Once resets were wired in, each real re-crash produced its own <code>componentDidCatch</code> call, giving an honest count of how many times users kept hitting the same still-broken condition — which is what actually justified escalating the backend fix's priority.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

