{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>What a boundary still can't catch — and how resets can misfire</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Error boundaries only catch errors thrown while React is rendering, in lifecycle methods, or in constructors of the components below them — not errors inside event handlers (an <code>onClick</code> that throws needs its own <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>), not errors in asynchronous code like a <code>setTimeout</code> or a fetch callback, and not errors in the boundary component itself. A recovery design that only wires up the render-phase boundary and assumes it also protects a broken click handler will leave that click handler crashing the whole page anyway.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This means "add error boundary recovery" is really two separate jobs: the boundary/<code>resetKeys</code> pattern covers render-phase failures in descendants, while event-handler and async failures need their own <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> (or a wrapping utility) that reports and recovers locally, often by just setting local component state rather than involving the boundary at all. Conflating the two leads to false confidence — "we have an error boundary" — while a crash in an async callback still takes down the page.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The other real footgun is a reset that fires too eagerly. <code>resetKeys</code> is a shallow comparison against the previous render's array — if you accidentally include a value that changes on every render (an inline object or array literal, a timestamp recomputed on each pass), the boundary resets on every single render, which defeats the point of catching anything: it immediately re-attempts and re-throws in a tight loop. The safe pattern ties a <code>resetKeys</code> entry to something that only changes when the actual upstream condition changes — a request ID, a websocket connection ID, a data version number — verified in DevTools by confirming the reset only fires when you'd expect it to, not on every parent re-render.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

